<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Games!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Show comprehension of basic commands used in the classroom.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of words related to school activities by responding with gestures, actions, or simple words in listening games like 'Bingo' (e.g., 'Show me what you like: games or puzzles?', and the students point to or say puzzles or games).
Can engage with instructions during interactive games (e.g., 'Point to your book.').
Can respond to simple oral prompts with one or two words related to school activities (e.g., 'Do you like games or math?' 'I like games.' 'I like math.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to introduce the lesson theme and target vocabulary, focusing on listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Begin with a "Hello Song" incorporating simple TPR actions (e.g., wave hello, clap hands). Then, show large, colorful flashcards of school activities: 'art', 'music', 'math', 'playground', 'science', 'English'. Point to each and say its name clearly. "Look! This is art." "This is music!" Repeat a few times, encouraging students to listen and look.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target phrase "I like [activity]" using expressive gestures and facial expressions. For example, hold up the 'games' flashcard, smile broadly, and say, "I like games!" (emphasizing /l/ in 'like' and /g/ in 'games'). Repeat with 'art', 'music', 'math'. Then model a simple command: "Point to games!" and demonstrate pointing. Model "Yes" with a nod and "No" with a shake of the head.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to 'music' flashcard) "Is this math?" (Students respond with 'No' or shake head).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to 'art' flashcard and smiles) "Do I like art?" (Students respond with 'Yes' or nod their head).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up 'games' flashcard) "What is this?" (Students can point, or say "Games!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language and content related to the scenario "I Like School!", primarily through listening activities with visual support for Kinder learners.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple teacher-led audio narrative/story with large, colorful picture cards (or a digital presentation with images) depicting different school activities and a 'teacher' character. The narrative will feature simple sentences using "I like..." and "Do you like...?" questions. Example characters: a 'teacher' and 'students' enjoying different activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening and interpreting simple oral messages related to school activities and preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher tells a short, engaging story about a school day, showing picture cards as the story progresses. "Hello, children! I am your teacher. I like school! What do I like to do? I like art. I like to color. (Show art flashcard, mime coloring.) I like music. I like to sing. (Show music flashcard, mime singing.) Do I like math? Yes, I like to count! (Show math flashcard, mime counting.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then introduces 'games' with enthusiasm: "And I really, really like games! Games are fun, games are exciting!" (Show games flashcard, emphasize /g/ sound).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening focus):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What does the teacher like?" (Show flashcards; students point to 'art', 'music', 'math', 'games'). "Does the teacher like school?" (Students nod 'yes').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher says, "Point to music!" or "Show me 'games'!" Students listen and point to the correct picture card.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher says, "Do I like art or science?" (showing both flashcards). Students point to 'art' based on the story.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities focused on developing listening accuracy, using target grammar and vocabulary. Responses are non-verbal or very limited verbal output for Kinder learners.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of 'Subject + verb' (I like...), 'Present simple' (I like games), and 'Yes/No questions' (Do you like it?), along with target vocabulary (teacher, art, playground, science, music, math, English, enjoy, choose, create, count, build, sing, share, look, write, color, interesting, funny, exciting, happy, sad, favorite, colorful).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Listen and Do" Commands (TPR):</b> The teacher gives commands using new vocabulary. Students listen and perform actions. "Listen! Color!" (Students mime coloring). "Listen! Sing!" (Students mime singing). "Listen! Count!" (Students mime counting). "Listen! Look at the teacher!" (Students look at the teacher). The teacher can then combine with preferences: "If you like games, clap your hands!" (Students listen and clap if they like games). "If you like music, sing a little song!" (Students listen and make a little singing sound).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – "Do You Like...?" Listening Game:</b> The teacher holds up two flashcards (e.g., 'art' and 'math'). Teacher asks, "Do you like art or math?" Students listen and point to their preferred activity on the flashcard. Teacher might then say, "Thumbs up if you like art, thumbs down if you don't!" to practice listening to "like" and "don't like." (Focus on recognition of 'like' and 'don't like').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Listen and Match" Activity:</b> Distribute worksheets with pictures of different school activities (art supplies, musical instruments, numbers, playground, etc.). The teacher calls out a word or simple phrase: "Find the music!" or "I like games!" or "Show me math!" Students listen and point to or circle the corresponding picture on their worksheet. This reinforces listening and phonemic awareness for words like 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on demonstrating comprehension through listening in a game context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> A listening-focused "School Activity Simon Says" game or a "Listen and Move" game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Teacher Says: I Like Games!"</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the rules: "If I say 'Teacher says...' you do the action. If I don't say 'Teacher says...' you don't do it!" (Emphasize listening carefully.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher starts giving commands related to school activities and preferences. Example commands:
                                <ul style="list-style-type: square; padding-left: 20px;">
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Teacher says, 'I like games!' (Students jump up and down.)"</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Teacher says, 'I like art!' (Students pretend to color.)"</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Teacher says, 'Point to the teacher!' (Students point to the teacher.)"</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Teacher says, 'Do you like music?' (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down.)"</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sing!" (Students should *not* sing because "Teacher says" was omitted, testing their careful listening.)</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Teacher says, 'Look at the playground picture!'" (Students look at the flashcard).</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher ensures that all target vocabulary (teacher, art, playground, science, music, math, English, enjoy, choose, create, count, build, sing, share, look, write, color, interesting, funny, exciting, happy, sad, favorite, colorful) and grammar (I like, Do you like?) are incorporated into the commands, requiring students to demonstrate listening comprehension through action.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on students' ability to listen to the full command and respond appropriately with actions or simple one-word verbalizations ('Yes'/'No' or the name of an activity if prompted).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check listening comprehension and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist (e.g., "Responds to 'Point to X'", "Responds to 'Do you like X?'", "Identifies 'like/don't like'").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "School Activity Bingo" (Listening Bingo)</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provide each student with a Bingo card featuring 4-6 pictures of school activities (e.g., 'art', 'music', 'math', 'games', 'playground', 'teacher').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher calls out words or short phrases: "Find 'music'!", "I like 'games'!", "Point to 'math'!" or "Do you like to 'sing'?" (students listen for the activity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and mark (with a counter or by coloring) the corresponding picture on their card. The first to get a line yells "Bingo!"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification of target vocabulary items when heard (pointing/marking correct picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Response to simple commands related to school activities (e.g., 'Show me games!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of 'like' and 'don't like' through gestures or simple responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and engagement in listening activities.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me with your thumb: Did you like our games today? (Thumbs up for yes, thumbs down for no)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like to listen to most? Point to the picture!" (Teacher holds up key flashcards again: 'games', 'music', 'art').</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear today that you liked?" (Students can point to flashcards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it fun to listen and play games today?" (Students nod or shake head).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students' listening comprehension? The TPR commands and the "Teacher Says" game were effective in getting non-verbal responses. The flashcards provided strong visual support for listening to new vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students struggled with differentiating between 'like' and 'don't like' when asked in a 'Yes/No' question format, requiring clearer gestures or more repetition. Need to ensure consistent and clear pronunciation of /l/ and /g/ sounds throughout.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will see these same vocabulary words written down. This listening foundation will help them recognize the words visually. I will prepare simple word cards to link sounds to written forms next.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>